## Technical Implementation

### Data Structures

#### SOME/IP Header (16 bytes)
```c
typedef struct SOMEIP_PACKED {
    uint16_t service_id;        // Service identifier (e.g., 0x1234)
    uint16_t method_id;         // Method or Event ID (e.g., 0x0001)
    uint32_t length;            // Length of payload + 8 bytes
    uint16_t client_id;         // Client identifier
    uint16_t session_id;        // Session identifier
    uint8_t  protocol_version;  // Always 0x01
    uint8_t  interface_version; // Service interface version
    uint8_t  message_type;      // REQUEST, RESPONSE, NOTIFICATION
    uint8_t  return_code;       // OK, ERROR codes
} someip_header_t;
```

**Design Rationale**:
- Packed structure ensures no padding
- Network byte order (big-endian) used on wire
- Length field includes header metadata (8 bytes)

#### Client Context
```c
typedef struct {
    Socket_t socket;
    BaseType_t active;
    someip_client_state_t client_state;
    
    // Phase 4: Event Group Subscriptions
    someip_eventgroup_subscription_t subscriptions[8];
    uint8_t subscription_count;
    
    // Lifecycle
    TickType_t last_activity_tick;
    TickType_t connect_tick;
    TaskHandle_t rx_task;
    
    // Thread Safety
    SemaphoreHandle_t mutex;
    
    // Statistics
    uint32_t messages_received;
    uint32_t notifications_sent;
} someip_client_ctx_t;
```

**Design Rationale**:
- Fixed-size subscription array (no dynamic allocation)
- Mutex protects subscription modifications
- Statistics for diagnostics

#### Event Group Definition
```c
typedef struct {
    uint16_t service_id;        // Which service (e.g., 0x1234)
    uint16_t eventgroup_id;     // Event group ID (e.g., 0x0001)
    someip_event_def_t events[8];  // Events in this group
    uint8_t event_count;
    uint32_t default_ttl_seconds;  // Default subscription TTL
} someip_eventgroup_def_t;
```

**Design Rationale**:
- Events grouped logically (e.g., "Diagnostics", "Telemetry")
- Client subscribes to entire group, not individual events
- TTL configured per event group

### Task Architecture

#### Task Hierarchy
```
someip_server_task (Priority: tskIDLE_PRIORITY + 2)
├── Accepts TCP connections
├── Allocates client slots
└── Spawns per-client RX tasks
    │
    └── someip_client_task (Priority: tskIDLE_PRIORITY + 1)
        ├── Receives SOME/IP messages
        ├── Handles Subscribe/Unsubscribe
        └── Sends ACK responses

someip_notification_task (Priority: tskIDLE_PRIORITY + 1)
├── Periodic timer (2 seconds)
└── Broadcasts notifications to all subscribed clients

someip_ttl_manager_task (Priority: tskIDLE_PRIORITY + 1)
├── Periodic timer (1 second)
└── Checks all subscriptions for expiration

sd_udp_task (Priority: tskIDLE_PRIORITY + 1)
├── Listens on UDP port 30490
└── Responds to FindService with OfferService
```

**Design Rationale**:
- Server task has highest priority (accept connections quickly)
- Notification and TTL tasks share priority (equal importance)
- Per-client RX tasks prevent one slow client from blocking others

### Threading Model

#### Synchronization Strategy
```c
// Client context access pattern
void someip_client_subscribe(...) {
    xSemaphoreTake(ctx->mutex, pdMS_TO_TICKS(100));
    // Modify subscription table
    xSemaphoreGive(ctx->mutex);
}

void someip_notification_task(...) {
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_CLIENTS; i++) {
        // Read-only check (no lock needed for active flag)
        if (g_someip_clients[i].active) {
            // Lock only when reading subscription state
            if (someip_client_is_subscribed(&g_someip_clients[i], ...)) {
                send_notification(&g_someip_clients[i]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Design Rationale**:
- Minimize lock duration (only hold mutex during subscription modification)
- Timeout on mutex acquisition (100ms) prevents deadlock
- Read-only checks don't require locks

### Memory Management

#### Static Allocation Strategy
```c
// Global tables (BSS section)
someip_client_ctx_t g_someip_clients[4];
someip_eventgroup_def_t eventgroup_registry[16];
someip_service_entry_t service_table[16];

// No calls to:
// - pvPortMalloc()
// - pvPortFree()
// - malloc()
// - free()
```

**Memory Budget**:
- Client table: 4 × 200 bytes = 800 bytes
- Event group registry: 16 × 100 bytes = 1600 bytes
- Service table: 16 × 16 bytes = 256 bytes
- Subscription tables: 4 × 8 × 20 bytes = 640 bytes
- **Total static: ~3.3 KB**

**Stack Budget**:
- Server task: 4 KB
- Client RX task: 4 KB (per client, max 4)
- Notification task: 2 KB
- TTL manager: 2 KB
- SD UDP task: 1 KB
- **Total stack: ~25 KB**

**Design Rationale**:
- Predictable memory usage (no heap fragmentation)
- Suitable for safety-critical systems
- Compile-time verification of memory bounds

